WEBVTT

00:00:10.666 --> 00:00:15.692
<b>Bobby:</b> A good maths teacher, I believe, has confidence in their students because

00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:20.408
if a student gets something wrong, if a teacher suddenly puts their hand on their heads and go,

00:00:20.407 --> 00:00:21.560
‘Oh God, what have you done?’,

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:25.886
then that child is going to lose their own ability to believe in themselves.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.512
A good teacher will be someone that will encourage them, but at the same time,

00:00:30.745 --> 00:00:32.475
try to bring the subject to life.

00:00:38.132 --> 00:00:42.962
Bobby: To get students to enjoy maths, one element is something called gamification,

00:00:43.321 --> 00:00:47.977
where young people nowadays, they love the idea of competitions and games.

00:00:47.998 --> 00:00:51.459
And again, they use social-media apps and they try and get streaks on Snapchat

00:00:51.545 --> 00:00:53.709
where they respond to each other every day.

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:55.734
So if you can capture that a little bit.

00:00:55.848 --> 00:00:59.825
Again with my students, we use apps and games where at the end of a lesson,

00:01:00.025 --> 00:01:04.599
we might have this, er, challenge where five questions, er, options A to D,

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:07.955
students work in twos or threes. And then I’ll put the question up,

00:01:08.063 --> 00:01:10.296
and they’ve got to quickly as a team work out the answer.

00:01:16.491 --> 00:01:20.075
Bobby: And imagine you’ve got a job interview, and your bus normally takes 30 minutes.

00:01:20.721 --> 00:01:23.273
Again, you might think on a normal day, you might just leave 30 minutes.

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:26.107
But actually, you might need to leave 15 minutes of waiting for the bus.

00:01:26.493 --> 00:01:30.461
And because we’re in busy London, lots of buses, there might be delays, breakdowns,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.532
you add, let’s say an additional 15 minutes. It takes an hour.

00:01:34.526 --> 00:01:38.648
And that conversation that we’ve had is actually practical maths in everyday use.

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:40.755
And you might think it’s just intuition,

00:01:41.008 --> 00:01:44.869
but you are using numbers to plan your journey, like all these people here.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:48.089
They’ve all got somewhere to be, whether it’s lunch or going to a work meeting,

00:01:48.235 --> 00:01:49.210
or picking up some shopping.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.978
All of them are planning and using numbers as part of their journey.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:56.454
So, the next time someone says to you, ‘I don’t use maths’ – yes, you do.

00:01:56.573 --> 00:01:58.580
All of us use maths in everyday life.